/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> setFillParadiddleLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "A paradiddle around the kit is the funk fill: the double inside each group hands the lead to the other hand, so the accents dance between drums without you steering them. It is the pad sheet \"Paradiddle Fill\" orchestrated.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Three bars of funk sixteenths: accented hi-hat sixteenths, backbeat on 2 and 4, kick on 1, the \"a\" of 1, the \"and\" of 2, 3 and the \"a\" of 3; bar 1 begins with the crash. In the fill bar the kick simplifies to 1 and 3. Line 1 plays the first paradiddle on the snare, then R L R R on the high tom and L R L L on the floor tom, one group per beat.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Line 2 accents the first note of every group and walks snare, high tom, mid tom, floor tom, so each accent opens a new drum. Line 4 is the inverted paradiddle on high tom and floor tom, line 5 the paradiddle-diddle on the snare with the last four on the floor tom, line 7 the double paradiddle over all four toms. Back to the funk kick right after the crash: that switch is the hard part. Toms accept any tom and the crash any crash, so a kit with fewer drums plays the sheet too: the mid tom and the second floor tom go to the nearest tom you have.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "Funk and fusion drumming, Steve Gadd, David Garibaldi: fills where the accents seem to jump between snare and toms.",
  ),
];
